                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Arr!  Ahoy, me mateys.  A pirate themed solitaire game awaits thee!\n\nIt's the \"3 Towers\" type of solitaire (the kind used in Faerie Solitaire) where you have to clear the board by clicking on cards with a number either higher or lower than the current one in the discard pile.  You can work up some pretty good chains by going back and forth like this: 5-6-7-6-5-4-3-2-3-2, etc.  you can use a cannonball as a wild card (it shoots off one of the cards on the board) and continue your chain.  \n\nThere's a story for those interested in that.  Something about boy meets girl, fall in love, but girl's father doesn't want her to marry boy.  Blah blah blah.  I've been skipping it since stories are not my thing.  But it's there for those who love stories.\n\nGameplay is solid, they give you enough cards in your deck to clear the board most of the time.  Of course, luck is a factor and sometimes you'll have to replay a level if you want all perfects.  Some cards are locked and  you have to do something to unlock them, and some have to be used twice to be cleared.  \n\nUnlike other solitaire games similar to this, there are no wild cards with numbers, just cannonballs that can be used on any card, but you don't get many cannonballs.  Most I've ever had at one time was four.  And there are no other special powers, like in that dragon one where you have to wait for the meter to fill up and you can use their power to slash a card off the board.  However you can buy upgrades like \"Remove four random cards at beginning of a level\" and it does it automitically.  So there are upgrades you can buy to help you out but they are not ones you activate yourself.  Mostly you gotta rely on your pirate wits, arrr!\n\nThe graphics are decent, pirate characters on the faces of the cards, even a monkey and a kraken!  Or you can switch to just the normal number card faces if you like.  There are nice touches like the journal log of your journey that tells the story, and after clearing a level the results show up on a piece of sailcloth with a spar above and ropes dangling.  Stuff like that really sets the scene nicely.\n\nMusic and sounds are okay.\n\nCan't think of anything else to say right now.  I've played a lot of different solitaire games and this one ranks high for me as a favorite and one I definitely would play again in the future!",
